[
    {
        "content": "<p>This might be a strange question, but it's one that we are somewhat stuck on in Iris-Lean. TL;DR: We want something that behaves like a <code>default_instance</code> but is tried first instead of last. </p>\n<p>In Iris-Lean we have a class </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">OFE</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">SI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">SIdx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SI</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">Dist</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">dist_eqv</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Equivalence</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Dist</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">eq_dist</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Dist</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">eq_dist'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Dist</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">dist_lt</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Dist</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Dist</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>99.99% of the time, Iris users have a default <code>SI</code> type they want to use for a section (generically over any <code>SIdx</code> or set it to be <code>Nat</code> or <code>Omega</code>) and in these cases they want it to be completely invisible. Ideally, there would be some way to specify a default value for the current section and then just forget about it, like you can do with canonical structures in Rocq. </p>\n<p>To emulate this behavior we have tried a few things: one option is making <code>SI</code> an <code>outParam</code> but this means being very careful about the <code>SIdx</code> instances in scope. An alternative <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/iris-lean/pull/576/changes#diff-78ca6b59cca1132357dad9fdad0d8d7ebb1ee743adad0703aa4000234472498eR17\">here</a>, where we write a command <code>local stepindex X</code> which registers a local <code>DefaultSI</code> instance, where </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DefaultSI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">SI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">outParam</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"kn\">private</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">mk</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">::</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">sidx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SIdx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SI</span>\n\n<span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">default_instance</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">reducible</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">dfltSIdx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">SI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DefaultSI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SI</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SIdx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">sidx</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I prefer this over the <code>outParam</code> version because it is easier to manage there only being a single <code>DefaultSI</code> instance in scope, and the <code>default_instance</code> seems to make nearly every annotation requirement dissapear. The problem is that this is quite slow: as shown in the PR above typeclass synthesis will try a ton of <code>SIdx</code> instanstations before hitting the default, and we are having trouble controlling this. In fact the number of aboreted instances blows up quadratically. Is there is a way to emulate <code>default_instance</code> behavior but have the default tried first? Alternatively, is this an <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/XY_problem\">#xy</a> for some other technique we might not have considered? Any tips appreciated.</p>",
        "id": 619280747,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1787749907
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Also, if this is known to just be a limitation of Lean, I'd like to know so I can move ahead and try to make the best of the <code>outParam</code> version.</p>",
        "id": 619282570,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1787750365
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>For this exact setup, register <code>dfltSIdx</code> both as a high-priority ordinary instance and as a default instance:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">default_instance</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">reducible</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">priority</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">high</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">dfltSIdx</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">SI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DefaultSI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SI</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SIdx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">sidx</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The ordinary registration puts this bridge ahead of the other <code>SIdx</code> candidates, while the default registration allows it to instantiate the unknown <code>SI</code>. Raising a <code>default_instance</code> priority alone would only reorder it relative to other defaults: default instances are considered only after ordinary search fails or gets stuck, as described in <a href=\"https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/latest/Type-Classes/Instance-Synthesis/\">Lean’s instance-synthesis documentation</a>.</p>",
        "id": 619287526,
        "sender_full_name": "Colin Bundschu",
        "timestamp": 1787751543
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks--I tried this before and I do not think it works. Here is the test I am running:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">set_option</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">trace</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Meta</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">synthInstance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">dist_equivalence</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">OFE</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SI</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Equivalence</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Dist</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">dist_eqv</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>And the traces for the existing version and your version still both try and abort a bunch of instances. From what I understand from the synthesis code, default instances as implemented are always tried last, no?</p>\n<div class=\"spoiler-block\"><div class=\"spoiler-header\">\n<p>Synthesis Traces</p>\n</div><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"spoiler-content\">\n<p>Old version with <code>def</code> and no <code>priority := high</code><br>\n<img alt=\"blowup-def-no-instance.png\" class=\"inline-image\" data-original-content-type=\"image/png\" data-original-dimensions=\"1226x1386\" data-original-src=\"/user_uploads/3121/aFrpCLq2jXW6H4jiiU55p_HX/blowup-def-no-instance.png\" src=\"/user_uploads/thumbnail/3121/aFrpCLq2jXW6H4jiiU55p_HX/blowup-def-no-instance.png/840x560.webp\"></p>\n<p>Proposed version with <code>instance</code> and  <code>priority := high</code><br>\n<img alt=\"blowup-high-instance.png\" class=\"inline-image\" data-original-content-type=\"image/png\" data-original-dimensions=\"1196x1476\" data-original-src=\"/user_uploads/3121/N_tN3O4uq9Q5MfLeN8p-0jS7/blowup-high-instance.png\" src=\"/user_uploads/thumbnail/3121/N_tN3O4uq9Q5MfLeN8p-0jS7/blowup-high-instance.png/840x560.webp\"></p>\n</div></div>",
        "id": 619303841,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1787755390
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>A <code>default_instance</code> seems to be able to assign metavariables that ordinary synthesis cannot, I think that's the reason this still aborts a bunch of times.</p>",
        "id": 619304518,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1787755535
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You’re right. I misread the interaction between the two registrations.</p>\n<p>Adding the ordinary high-priority registration only changes ordering within ordinary instance search. Its application to <code>SIdx ?SI</code> still becomes stuck, while the <code>default_instance</code> registration is considered only afterward. Lean’s implementation explicitly runs ordinary synthesis to a fixed point before trying defaults (<a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/blob/master/src/Lean/Elab/SyntheticMVars.lean#L172-L176\">source</a>).</p>\n<p>AFAIK there is currently no priority annotation that means \"default instance, but before ordinary search.\" My read is that avoiding these aborted searches requires determining <code>SI</code> earlier using an <code>outParam</code>, inserting it through the existing <code>stepindex%</code> elaborator/notation, or restructuring the APIs.</p>",
        "id": 619305439,
        "sender_full_name": "Colin Bundschu",
        "timestamp": 1787755737
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>So yeah I guess what I am saying is that I agree with your XY assessment: I think you might be shaving the wrong yak. I would be willing to bet this problem is a symptom of a deeper structural issue, although I am not sure I can pinpoint exactly what needs to be fixed. I'm sorry my original answer was not of more help, I'll give this a think and let you know if anything comes to mind.</p>",
        "id": 619307196,
        "sender_full_name": "Colin Bundschu",
        "timestamp": 1787756154
    },
    {
